timestamp=2026-09-26T19:37:34.163Z level=INFO run=01b4c3ae message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 - <<'PY'\np='shield-kitty-fail2ban.local'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"\"\"[recidive]\nenabled  = true\nport     = all\nfilter   = recidive\nlogpath  = /var/log/fail2ban.log\nmaxretry = 3\nfindtime = 1d\nbantime  = -1\"\"\",\n\"\"\"[recidive]\n# ⚠ BOOTSTRAP ORDER MATTERS. recidive reads /var/log/fail2ban.log — fail2ban's\n# OWN log. On a machine where fail2ban has never run, that file does not exist,\n# and fail2ban ABORTS THE ENTIRE STARTUP with:\n#\n#     ERROR  Have not found any log file for 'recidive' jail.\n#\n# which leaves the other three jails dead too. So recidive ships DISABLED here.\n# Enable it on the SECOND start, once the log exists:\n#\n#   sudo systemctl start fail2ban                 # first start, creates the log\n#   sudo sed -i 's/^enabled  = false/enabled  = true/' /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/shield-kitty.local\n#   sudo systemctl restart fail2ban                # now recidive can read it\n#\nenabled  = false\nport     = all\nfilter   = recidive\nlogpath  = /var/log/fail2ban.log\nmaxretry = 3\nfindtime = 1d\nbantime  = -1\"\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint(\"  [✓] recidive now defaults to disabled with documented enable steps\")\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
