timestamp=2026-09-26T21:15:12.023Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git commit -q -m \"fix: rewrite integration for arpwatch 3.9, which has no exec hook\n\nThe service exited 1 in a restart loop because every flag I used belongs to\nthe original LBL arpwatch, not the 3.9 build in Arch 'extra'. 3.9 has no -a\n(exec hook), no -u, no -c, and takes -i INTERFACE instead of a positional\nargument. Its real options:\n\n    [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface] [-P pidfile]\n    [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email] [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]]\n    [-r file]\n\nSo there is no way to have arpwatch exec a program. Detection now reads the\ndatafile instead, and the exec hook is replaced by a scanner on a timer:\n\n  arpwatch-spoof.service   -F -i wlo1 -f .../arp.dat -s -q -N -z\n                           -F foreground for systemd, -s no email (no MTA),\n                           -q no syslog spam, -N no bogon reports (this is a\n                           /8, so most traffic looks like a bogon), -z no\n                           0.0.0.0 reports, which arpwatch(8) recommends for\n                           busy DHCP networks\n  arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh   polls the datafile, alerts on one IP claimed by\n                           more than one DISTINCT MAC\n  ...-scan.service/.timer  every 2 minutes\n\nThe old arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh is kept: it remains the manual test path for\nproving the notify chain without waiting for real LAN traffic.\n\nThree bugs found by testing:\n\n- Counting MACs by looking for a comma was wrong. arpwatch can list the same\n  hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those gives\n  'mac,mac' -- a comma, but one device, so it false-alarmed. Now counts unique\n  values.\n- When anomalies cleared, the scan exited before writing state, leaving it\n  stale. A spoof that resolved and later returned would be swallowed as\n  'already seen'. State is now cleared, and a returning spoof re-alerts.\n- Installer, scanner and unit disagreed on the datafile name (arpwatch.dat vs\n  arp.dat). Unified on arp.dat, which is also arpwatch's own default.\n\nAlerts are deduplicated against a state file, so a persistent anomaly alerts\nonce rather than once every 2 minutes.\n\nVerified 12 cases: clean data, same-MAC-different-case, genuine 2-MAC and\n3-MAC spoofs, one MAC across two IPs (deliberately not a signal), loopback,\noptional hostname field, empty datafile, missing datafile, custom\nBDE_SPOOF_IGNORE, and the full dedup lifecycle (new / unchanged / escalated /\nresolved / returned). 5000-row datafile scans in 18ms.\"" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
