timestamp=2026-09-26T21:17:51.357Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git commit -q -m \"fix: drop -q, which arpwatch 3.9 documents but rejects\n\nThe service was still printing usage and exiting 1. The cause was -q.\n\narpwatch(8) lists -q in the synopsis [-CdFNpqsvzZ] and gives it an OPTIONS\nentry, but the 3.9 binary rejects it: it prints the usage message and exits 1.\nRan every letter in that synopsis to check -- -C -d -F -N -p -s -v -z -Z are\nall accepted, and -q is the only one that is not. So this is a bug in the man\npage, not an obscure flag, and the page cannot be trusted on its own here.\n\narpwatch prints its usage before attempting to open the raw socket, so a bad\nflag is detectable unprivileged and instantly. Added that as a parse-check in\ninstall-arpwatch-spoof.sh, reading the flags from a single ARPWATCH_ARGS\nvariable shared with the unit so the two cannot drift again. It aborts the\ninstall with a pointer to the doc/binary disagreement rather than leaving a\nservice in a restart loop where the only evidence is in the journal.\n\nVerified the guard: it rejects the old -q command and accepts the current one.\n\nExecStart is now: arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -N -z\nWithout -q, arpwatch's own flip-flop reports go to the journal, which is a\nuseful audit trail since 3.9 cannot deliver them anywhere else.\"" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-09-26T21:17:51.358Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git log --oneline" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-09-26T21:17:51.358Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="head -1" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
