timestamp=2026-09-26T21:28:52.678Z level=INFO run=01b4c3ae message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git add config/zen-hardening/fix-sudoers-permissions.sh" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-09-26T21:28:52.678Z level=INFO run=01b4c3ae message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git -c user.name=opencode -c user.email=opencode@localhost commit -q -m \"Add sudoers permission repair that cannot activate an unreviewed grant\n\nsudo reports 'bad permissions, should be mode 0440' for several files in\n/etc/sudoers.d, including 'opencode' -- not a stock Arch file, created\nduring this session.\n\nThe obvious repair, chmod 0440 on everything, is wrong. Those files are\nignored by sudo precisely because their permissions are too open, so\nchanging the mode makes sudo start honouring them. If one contains a\nNOPASSWD rule, the chmod activates passwordless root and removes the\nevidence in the same step.\n\nThis classifies each file first. Only Defaults-only files (the sudo\npackage defaults: editor, insults, pwfeedback) are activated at 0440.\nAnything containing NOPASSWD, an ALL=(...) ALL style grant, or anything\nunrecognised is copied to a root-only 0600 quarantine directory and\nremoved from /etc/sudoers.d, so it stops being a sudo surface while\nstaying readable for review.\n\nGroup/other write bits are stripped before classification regardless,\nsince a writable sudoers file is a live root-escalation path and\noutranks every other concern. Nothing is deleted; the script prints the\noriginal contents of anything it moves and how to restore it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" 2>&1" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
