timestamp=2026-09-26T21:50:53.545Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git add -A" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-09-26T21:50:53.545Z level=INFO run=501861cf message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="git commit -q -m \"fix: installer left the old collector running, so -P never took effect\n\n'enable --now' only starts a unit that is not already running. The collector was\nalready up with the previous ExecStart, so it kept running with the old flags:\nno -D, so ethercodes.dat was still never read, and no -P, so\n/run/arpwatch-spoof.pid was never created. The scan unit then SIGHUPs a pidfile\nthat does not exist, the subshell fails silently behind '2>/dev/null', and the\nscanner goes back to reading a file arpwatch only rewrites every 15 minutes --\nthe precise failure the previous commit fixed, reintroduced by the installer.\n\nNow enable + restart, and afterwards the installer checks the pidfile actually\nappeared and warns loudly if not, because a missing pidfile degrades to silent\nstaleness rather than to any visible error.\n\nAlso corrected the closing banner, which still told the user to inspect 'the raw\nargs' if a real event looked wrong. That advice belongs to the exec-hook design\nthat arpwatch 3.9 cannot do; there are no hook args any more. Detection reads\narp.dat, so the useful next step is running the scan unit once and reading\n/var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log.\"" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
